<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krishnadevaraya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1509–1529</span> · Also known as Krishna Deva Raya, Krishnadeva Raya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Krishnadevaraya was emperor of Vijayanagara from 1509 to 1529, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuluva-dynasty">Tuluva dynasty</a>, and the most celebrated ruler of the empire. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a>, took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur-doab">Raichur Doab</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> in 1520 and conquered coastal Andhra from the Gajapatis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. A poet in Telugu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, he patronised the poets known as the Ashtadiggajas and built widely at Hampi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya.jpg" alt="Krishnadevaraya" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ravitheja Kumar Reddy C · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinnadevi,_Krishnadevaraya,_Tirumaladevi_statues_at_Chandragiri_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
